-- Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia, batch 6: vehicles and power armor.
-- Eighteen rows with their M.D.C.-by-location tables and weapon lists: the
-- five Novyet vehicles, the Heavy M.D.C. Snowmobile, the two Tek bikes, the
-- Landflier, the Warrior Assault Hoversled (light and heavy), the War
-- Chariot, the War Wagon, the Warthrone and the Wingrider Flying Wing
-- (printed 144-164), and three suits: the Wingrider Power Armor (164), the
-- War-Knight Power Armor (93-94) and the MM-61 Explorer Exoframe (176-177).
-- See apps/character-creator/docs/surveys/warlords-of-russia.md.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/add-warlords-of-russia-vehicles.sql
--
-- EVERY NUMBER WAS READ OFF A 130 DPI RENDER and checked against the render
-- again by book-reconcile: these pages set each stat block beside line art
-- and the text layer returns the lines out of order. 18 rows checked; the
-- only corrections were two page citations.
--
-- CONVENTIONS:
--   cost is the lowest printed price; the others are in cost_note. A printed
--     range or roll stores its low end, with the range in the note.
--   The Warrior Assault Hoversled prints a light and a heavy model in one
--     block and is two rows. The heavy row's cost, 1,500,000, is DERIVED: the
--     book prints the light price and "add 600,000 credits for the heavy
--     version". Its cost_note says so.
--   The Explorer-Sku and the War Wagon print two main-body sections and no
--     single Main Body line. mdc_main_body holds the forward section (230,
--     400), the one whose loss stops the vehicle; both sections are locations.
--   The War-Knight Power Armor's main body is printed as a roll, 2D6x10+110;
--     130 is stored. Its block prints no weapon damage, so it has no weapon
--     rows.
--   No price is printed for the War Chariot, the War Wagon or the Wingrider
--     Power Armor: cost is NULL.
--
-- STORED AS PRINTED, and said so in the rows. The book's metric conversions
-- on these pages are often wrong (2.3 kg for 2.3 m on four vehicles; a
-- "1200 feet (610 km)" laser; a 150 foot ceiling given as 76.2 m), several
-- M.D.C. lines covering two items print no "each", the 'Borgbike prints two
-- fuel ranges, and the War Wagon's laser series reads "GU-SO".

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles
  (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, cost, cost_note, description, source_book)
VALUES
  ('novyet-arctic-hoverbike', 'Novyet Arctic Hoverbike', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One.', 'One human-sized passenger up to 7.6 feet (printed ''2.3 kg'', meaning 2.3 m) and 500 pounds (225 kg); suits partial and Light Machine ''Borgs, full conversion cyborgs and Heavy Machines may be too large and/or heavy.', NULL, 'Hover stationary to 100 mph (160 km); cruising speed typically around 60 mph (96.5 km). Thruster assisted leaps 30 feet (9 m) high and 100 feet (30.5 m) across. Flight ceiling 150 feet (printed ''76.2 m'', which is 250 feet); VTOL capable.', NULL, 'Height 4 feet (1.2 m) body; width 3.6 feet (1.1 m), wide at the rear, tapers a foot (0.3 m) toward the front; length 8 feet (2.4 m). Landing gear is three skis, two at the rear sides and one centered under the front.', 'One ton', 135, 90000, '90,000 to 120,000 credits (higher in remote regions) for the standard alcohol or gasoline mix internal combustion engine with a 300 mile (480 km) range; good availability. 1.1 million credits for the nuclear powered bike with a 10 year life and effectively unlimited range; poor availability. The low end is stored.', 'Novyet Manufacturing''s winter hovercycle, with an enclosed heated cockpit, heated locks, defrosting and a heat-circulation system that lets it run at -130 F (-90 C) and, with only minor stalling, down to -200 F (-129 C); its muffled soft-flow jets disturb little snow, which is why it is slower than ordinary hovercycles. Power is internal combustion (alcohol and/or gasoline mix, 300 miles/480 km) or nuclear (unlimited range). Cargo is minimal (rifle, survival kit, backpack, a few small items), plus 200 pounds (90 kg) strapped outside and up to 300 pounds (136 kg) towed. Life support: air circulation and cleaning with gas filtration and toxic warning, sealable with one hour of internal oxygen, withstands 200 Rads. No weapons are printed. The Sovietski has 20,000 with another 20,000 on order; 5% are nuclear powered.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.144-145'),
  ('novyet-snow-jetsled', 'Novyet Snow-Jetsled', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One.', 'One human-sized driver and one human-sized passenger up to 7.6 feet (printed ''2.3 kg'', meaning 2.3 m) and a total of 600 pounds (270 kg); suits partial and Light Machine ''Borgs, full conversion cyborgs and Heavy Machines are usually too large and/or heavy.', 'Top speed 150 mph (240 km); cruising speed usually around 70 mph (112.6 km). Woodlands or rugged terrain reduce maximum speed by 50-70%. Thruster assisted leaps 15 feet (4.6 m) high and 50 feet (15.2 m) across.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 2.5 feet (0.76 m); width 3 feet (0.9 m); length 6 feet (1.8 m)', '1000 pounds (450 kg)', 100, 45000, '45,000 to 60,000 credits (higher in remote regions) for the standard alcohol or gasoline mix turbine engine with a 250 mile (400 km) range; excellent availability. One million credits for the nuclear powered version with a 10 year life and effectively unlimited range; rare. The low end is stored.', 'A low, sleek ski-mounted snowmobile driven by computer-controlled vector jets with a retractable exhaust baffle and forward braking jets, built for open ground and needing a trail in very rugged or mountainous country. Power is a liquid fuel turbine/turbojet (250 miles/400 km) or nuclear (effectively unlimited). Taking more than 30 M.D.C. in a single crushing fall or attack knocks out one jet engine and cuts speed by 30%. Cargo is minimal (rifle, survival kit, backpack), plus 200 pounds (90 kg) strapped outside and another 200 pounds (90 kg) towed, though towing cuts speed by 20% and gives -10% to the pilot skill. No weapons are printed. The Sovietski has 40,000; 2% are nuclear powered.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.145-146'),
  ('novyet-landcrawler-sku', 'Novyet Landcrawler-Sku', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two.', 'Space for two passengers; another human-sized passenger can squeeze in if necessary, and two could also ride on top of the vehicle.', 'Top speed 72 mph (120 km), cruising 42 mph (70 km); half under treacherous conditions.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 6 feet (1.8 m); width 5 feet (1.5 m); length 9 feet (2.7 m)', '3 tons', 130, 185000, '185,000 Universal credits. Add one million for a nuclear powered Landcrawler, which also adds 40 M.D.C. As many as two light weapon systems can be built in at extra cost, and up to 80 M.D.C. added at 10,000 credits per 20 M.D.C. No availability printed.', 'A compact exploration and personnel carrier with two treads at the rear and two large M.D. front wheels on independent suspension (swappable for skis in deep snow), small enough for narrow streets, trails and stairs and able to climb inclines up to 55 degrees (30 to 45 is optimum). Power is gasoline or electric/solar batteries (each 250 miles/400 km per tank or charge) or nuclear; the solar version recharges outdoors at 10 minutes of daylight per mile. Cargo is up to 1,000 pounds (450 kg) in side saddlebag compartments and it can pull up to three tons. Sensors: digital HUD with passive nightvision and auto-range-finding, 1,500 feet (457 m) each, and radio to 10 miles (16 km). Life support: 16 hours of internal oxygen with gas filtration, withstands 500 Rads. No weapons are printed as standard. The Sovietski has 10,000 with another 2000 on order; 30% are nuclear powered.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.146-147'),
  ('novyet-explorer-sku', 'Novyet Explorer-Sku', 'rifts', 'vehicle', '2-4.', '6-8 passengers comfortably, 14 cramped, depending on the configuration and use of the rear cabin/cargo bay.', 'Top 70 mph (112 km), typical cruising speed 40 mph (64 km). Deep snow and treacherous terrain may reduce speed to under 20 mph (32 km). Pulling a load halves maximum speed.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 8 feet (2.4 m); width 12 feet (3.6 m); length 22 feet (6.7 m)', '7 tons', 230, 565000, '565,000 Universal credits for a basic cargo Explorer with a liquid fuel system. Add one million for nuclear power (+40 additional M.D.C.). Add 20,000 for fully outfitted living quarters for eight. Add 100,000 for the laboratory and research cabin. As many as four light weapon systems can be built in at extra cost. No availability printed.', 'A big four-tread hauler with a small forward cab and a rear bay that can carry cargo, be fitted as an eight-seat living cabin (computer stations, storage, 20 gallon water cooler, mini-grill, bathroom) or, in 65% of them, as a field laboratory with 3-4 bunks, refrigeration, specimen storage, recording and communications/sensor stations and a kitchen with a 100 gallon water tank. Power is a liquid fuel combustion engine with an independent laboratory generator (700 miles/1120 km) or nuclear, split about 50/50. It carries 20 tons and pulls another 20. Life support: gas filtration and toxic warning, cab and rear cabin seal independently with an 8 hour oxygen supply and air recycling for roughly 14-18 days, withstands 400 Rads. Sensors: digital HUD with passive nightvision and auto-range-finding (1,500 feet/457 m each) plus standard large vehicle sensors. The main body is printed as two sections, cab 230 and rear cabin/lab 310. Most street versions carry no weapon or one; the Sovietski has 2,300 fitted with one MK Saber Quad-Rail Gun Turret, one Kharkov M9 Manned Rail Gun Turret and a mini-missile launcher (64 missiles), 60% nuclear and 70% lab versions.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.147-148'),
  ('novyet-bear-atv', 'Novyet Bear ATV', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One driver.', '6-9 additional human-sized passengers (the text says it seats six comfortably with room for two in the pilot''s area).', '95 mph (152 km) maximum, typical cruising speed 50 mph (80 km). Handles well on ice and snow up to three feet (0.9 m) deep; deeper snow risks getting stuck.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 10 feet (2.1 m) in the front, 7.6 feet (2.3 m) in the rear [as printed; the figures do not agree]; tires four feet (1.2 m) in diameter; width 8 feet (2.4 m); length 18 feet (5.4 m)', '6 tons', 135, 136000, '136,000 Universal credits for a basic Bear with a liquid fuel system. Add one million for nuclear power (+40 additional M.D.C.). Add 10,000 credits per 20 additional M.D.C., up to 80 extra maximum. As many as three light weapon systems can be built in at extra cost. No availability printed.', 'A thick-bodied four-wheel-drive ATV on wide balloon tires, larger than the Big Boss ATV and taller and wider at the front, with posi-traction favoring the front wheels so that hill climbs are easier. Power is a liquid fuel system (500 miles/800 km) or nuclear. It carries up to two tons (900 kg) and pulls five more, and comes with a tow hitch (100 feet/30.5 m) and a built-in winch equal to a robot P.S. of 30. Life support: air circulation and cleaning with gas filtration and toxic warning, handles 300 Rads; sensors are nothing special apart from a Cyber-Link. Most street versions have no weapons or one; the Sovietski has 2,100 equipped with one Kharkov M9 Manned Rail Gun Turret and a mini-missile launcher (32 missiles), 33% nuclear powered.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.148-149'),
  ('heavy-mdc-snowmobile', 'Heavy M.D.C. Snowmobile', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One human-sized driver.', 'One passenger; driver (and passenger) up to 7.6 feet (printed ''2.3 kg'', meaning 2.3 m) and 500 pounds (225 kg); suits partial and Light Machine ''Borgs, full conversion cyborgs and Heavy Machines may be too large and/or heavy.', '100 mph (160 km); cruising speed typically around 50 mph (80.5 km). Leaps six feet (1.8 m) high and 12 feet (3.6 m) across.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 3 feet (0.9 m); width 3.6 feet (1.1 m); length 6 feet (1.8 m)', '500 pounds (225 kg)', 85, 26000, '26,000 to 40,000 credits (higher in remote regions) for the standard liquid fuel engine; good availability. 925,000 to 1.1 million credits for the nuclear powered vehicle with a 10 year life and effectively unlimited range; poor availability. As many as two light weapon systems can be built in at extra cost, and up to 50 M.D.C. added at 5,000 credits per 10 M.D.C. Sold by the Black Market and Poles. The low end is stored.', 'A dependable heavy-duty snowmobile sold by the Black Market and the Poles, less cold-proof than the Novyet machines. Below -30 F (-34 C) the locks and ignition have a 01-80% chance of freezing and it runs sluggishly (-30% speed, -10% skill); at -50 F (-46 C) or colder the engine freezes within five minutes of being switched off and there is a 01-50% chance of a stall (roll every 15 minutes) that cannot be restarted until warmed to at least 32 F / 0 C. Going faster than 40 mph (64 km) over loose or fresh snow on a slope carries a 01-60% chance of setting off an avalanche or slide. Power is internal combustion (alcohol and/or gasoline mix, 200 miles/320 km) or nuclear (unlimited). Cargo is minimal (rifle, winter survival kit, backpack, a few small items), plus 100 pounds (45 kg) strapped outside and up to 300 pounds (136 kg) towed. No weapons are printed as standard.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.149-150'),
  ('tek-12-yy110-bushbike', 'Tek-12 YY110 Bushbike', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One pilot.', 'Can hold another ''Borg sized passenger. Capacity line: one human-sized driver up to 7.6 feet (printed ''2.3 kg'', meaning 2.3 m) and 500 pounds (225 kg); suits partial and Light Machine ''Borgs, full conversion cyborgs and Heavy Machines may be too large and/or heavy.', '137 mph (219 km) maximum, typical cruising speed 60 mph (96.5 km). Rugged, uneven terrain reduces best speed by 30-50%. Leaps 10 feet (3 m) high and 30 feet (9 m) across.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 3 feet (0.9 m); width 2 feet (0.6 m); length 7 feet (2.1 m)', '1,600 pounds (727 kg)', 135, 32000, '32,000 credits for a stripped down (printed ''tripped down''; no weapons) version and 65,000 credits for the fully loaded combat version with a liquid fuel or electric engine. Add 900,000 for a nuclear powered bike. Add 1500 credits for a sidecar or trailer with three M.D.C., or 10,000 credits for one with 20 M.D.C. Add 40,000 credits for the Blunderbuss launcher. No availability printed. The lowest price is stored.', 'A heavy solid-tire combat motorcycle (model YY110, class overland motorcycle) with one reverse gear, dual shock absorption and the ability to climb rocky slopes up to 50 degrees; the Reavers of the War Camps use it widely for patrol, reconnaissance and combat, as do adventurers and mercenaries. Power is internal combustion (alcohol and/or gasoline mix, 350 miles/560 km), electric (275 miles/440 km) or nuclear (unlimited). A small rear compartment holds a rifle, backpack and a few small items, 200 pounds (90 kg) more can be strapped on, and it can pull 500 pounds (225 kg) in a sidecar or two-wheeled trailer at -10% speed and -5% piloting. Features: communicator (10 miles/16 km), Cyber-Link and a passive nightvision optic screen with a 1,000 foot (304 m) range. The rider can fire hand-held weapons one-handed while driving at -20% piloting, and many bikes carry a spare tire on the back.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.150-151'),
  ('tek-20-yy210-borgbike', 'Tek-20 YY210 ''Borgbike', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One pilot.', 'May take up to one additional passenger of ''Borg size or smaller. Capacity line: one giant-sized driver, 7-10 feet (2.1 to 3 m) tall, and 1,500 pounds (675 kg); suits Light and Heavy Machines and most full conversion ''Borgs.', '204 mph (327 km) top speed without thrusters; cruising 85 mph (136 km). Rugged, uneven terrain reduces best speed by 30-50%. Backward at 1/4 top speed. Jet boost: up to 350 mph (560 km) in two seconds (one melee action), held for up to 45 seconds (three melee rounds), then one minute before re-engaging. Normal leaps 8 feet (2.4 m) high and 20 feet (6 m) across; jet thruster leaps 30 feet (9 m) high and 100 feet (30.5 m) across.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 4 feet (1.2 m) to handlebars, 6 feet (1.8 m) overall; width 3 feet (.6 m) overall [as printed; 3 feet is about 0.9 m]; length 11 feet (3.3 m)', '2.1 tons', 216, 185000, '185,000 credits for the fully loaded combat version with a liquid fuel engine, or 1.5 million for a fully loaded nuclear powered bike. Sidecars and trailers are not possible (-30% piloting and 01-50% chance of breaking off if one is used). No availability printed.', 'A massive armored combat motorcycle (model YY210, class overland motorcycle) built for Heavy Machines and full conversion cyborgs, ridden by the War Camps'' cyborgs and Reaver Cavalry and by large D-Bees. Its jet boosters demand a cyber-linked pilot (otherwise -60% to pilot skill, with a likely crash); jet leaps need only 6-10 seconds of thrust. Power is liquid fuel (30% of bikes; range printed as 250 miles/400 km under Speed and as 450 miles under Statistical Data; 60 liter tank, about 10 credits a liter; every 15 seconds of jet thrust cuts range by 10 miles/16 km) or nuclear (70%, unlimited). Attackers are -3 to strike the bike or driver when it is airborne or above 200 mph (320 km), and the driver is -10% piloting above that speed. A small rear compartment holds a rifle, e-clips, backpack and small items; it can pull 1500 pounds (675 kg) at -10% speed and -5% piloting. Features: communicator (10 miles/16 km), Cyber-Link, short-range radar (5 miles/8 km), bullhorn (300 ft/91 m), spotlight (200 ft/61 m) and passive nightvision screen (1,000 feet/304 m). Hand-held weapons can be fired one-handed while driving at -20% piloting.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.151-153'),
  ('landflier', 'Landflier', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One human-sized driver.', 'One passenger; riders up to 7.6 feet (2.3 m) tall and 1,500 pounds (675 kg), suitable for humans and full conversion cyborgs alike. A second small passenger may be squeezed in, uncomfortably, for a -10% piloting penalty.', 'Ground/wheeled speed 120 mph (192 km).', 'Hover speed 200 mph (320 km) maximum; cruising speed typically around 60 mph (96.5 km). Hover leaps up to 20 feet (printed ''1.8 m'', which is 6 feet) high and 50 feet (15.2 m) across without actually getting airborne.', NULL, 'Height 3 feet (0.9 m); width 3.6 feet (1.1 m) overall; length 12 feet (3.6 m)', '1500 pounds (675 kg)', 120, 64000, 'Black Market cost 64,000 to 85,000 credits (higher in remote regions) for the standard liquid fuel engine; poor availability (only about 20% have fuel engines). 1.2 million credits for the nuclear powered version with a 12 year life and effectively unlimited range; fair availability. As many as two light weapon systems can be built in at extra cost, and up to 60 M.D.C. added at 5,000 credits per 10 M.D.C. The low end is stored.', 'A sturdy two-seat (tandem) all-terrain machine sold by the Black Market and the Poles that runs on three retractable wheels (two rear, one front) or hovers on underbelly jets, a nose jet and a pair of tail thrusters; explorers, couriers, adventurers and mercenaries favor it. Power is a liquid fuel engine (300 miles/482 km) or nuclear (unlimited). The rear cargo bay takes a winter survival kit, four backpacks, two bedrolls and some small items, mounts ahead of it hold two rifles, another 500 pounds (225 kg) can be strapped outside or slung in saddlebags, and it can pull up to 1000 pounds (450 kg).', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.153'),
  ('warrior-assault-hoversled-light', 'Warrior AH Assault Hoversled (Light)', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two (or four, see above); pilot/gunner, gunner/navigator.', 'Built for two riders in all; a third fits under cramped conditions at -10% piloting. The Crew line prints ''or four'' while the text above it prints three.', '220 mph (352 km) on snow and/or ice, though under 150 mph (240 km) is more usual; 40% of normal on dry ground, which damages the skis and undercarriage. Same for both models.', 'Top speed 306 mph (490 km); cruising 120 mph (192 km); maximum altitude 300 feet (91.5 m). Same for both models.', NULL, 'Height 3 feet (0.9 m); width 3.6 feet (1.1 m); length 7 feet (2.1 m)', '2 tons', 212, 900000, 'Black Market: 900,000 credits liquid fuel; 1.1 million electric/solar; 2.2 million nuclear. The low end is stored. Officially issued only to Warlord Camp members; others use them illegally. A noncombat gasoline version with no built-in weapons and 105 M.D.C. main body costs 380,000 credits, fair to poor availability.', 'The smaller of the two AH-sled models, a snow-and-air hover assault sled sized for ordinary humans and partial ''Borgs and fielded in huge numbers by every Warlord''s Camp. VTOL capable. Power: liquid fuel with a 325 mile (520 km) range, electric battery and solar with 210 miles (336 km), or nuclear with effectively unlimited range and a 10 year life. Attackers are -1 to strike for every 50 mph (80 km) the sled is travelling, on top of the -4 for small targets; riders firing hand-held weapons take the same -1 per 50 mph, but built-in weapons do not. Depleting the main body shuts the sled down. Cargo: minimal space, but it can carry or pull 1000 pounds (450 kg) beyond the riders (estimated 500 to 800 lbs/225 to 360 kg). Sensors: HUD, short-range directional communications 20 miles (32 km), bullhorn 400 feet (122 m), sensor antenna for a mini-radar that tracks up to 20 aerial targets, extended radio (adds 20 miles/32 km) and Cyber-Link. The passenger can use hand-held weapons or the rear gun. A weaponless noncombat knock-off also exists in the gasoline model only: 105 M.D.C. main body, most other locations as the light sled.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.154-156'),
  ('warrior-assault-hoversled-heavy', 'Warrior AH Assault Hoversled (Heavy)', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two (or four, see above); pilot/gunner, gunner/navigator.', 'Built for two riders in all; a third fits under cramped conditions at -10% piloting. The Crew line prints ''or four'' while the text above it prints three.', '220 mph (352 km) on snow and/or ice, though under 150 mph (240 km) is more usual; 40% of normal on dry ground, which damages the skis and undercarriage. Same for both models.', 'Top speed 306 mph (490 km); cruising 120 mph (192 km); maximum altitude 300 feet (91.5 m). Same for both models.', NULL, 'Height 4 feet (1.2 m); width 5 feet (1.5 m); length 10 feet (3 m)', '3.6 tons', 275, 1500000, 'The book prints light-sled prices (900,000 credits liquid fuel; 1.1 million electric/solar; 2.2 million nuclear) and says to add 600,000 credits for the heavy version regardless of power system: 1.5 million, 1.7 million and 2.8 million. The stored figure is that sum for liquid fuel, not a printed number. Officially issued only to Warlord Camp members; others use them illegally. The 380,000 credit noncombat gasoline version is described against the light sled.', 'The larger AH-sled model, about 20% bigger, built for full conversion cyborgs (Heavy Machines) and large, heavy D-Bees. It has three rear jets and six hoverjets where the light sled has two and four. VTOL capable. Power: liquid fuel with a 400 mile (640 km) range, electric battery and solar with 300 miles (480 km), or nuclear with effectively unlimited range and a 10 year life. Attackers are -1 to strike for every 50 mph (80 km) the sled is travelling, on top of the -4 for small targets; riders firing hand-held weapons take the same -1 per 50 mph, but built-in weapons do not. Depleting the main body shuts the sled down. Cargo: minimal space, but it can carry or pull 1000 pounds (450 kg) beyond the riders (estimated 500 to 800 lbs/225 to 360 kg). Sensors: HUD, short-range directional communications 20 miles (32 km), bullhorn 400 feet (122 m), sensor antenna for a mini-radar that tracks up to 20 aerial targets, extended radio (adds 20 miles/32 km) and Cyber-Link. The passenger can use hand-held weapons or the rear gun. A weaponless noncombat knock-off also exists in the gasoline model only: 105 M.D.C. main body, most other locations as the light sled.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.154-156'),
  ('warlord-war-chariot', 'Warlord War Chariot', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One pilot, plus 4-5 Reavers or partial or full conversion ''Borgs hanging from the outer cage.', 'As a troop transport, 10 additional warriors can cling to the cage; in combat more than five get in each other''s way and lack protection.', 'Top speed 110 mph (176 km); cruising 60 mph (96 km).', NULL, 'Not possible underwater, but it can drive through water up to four feet (1.2 m) deep.', 'Height 12 feet (3.7 m) to the assault platform, wheel height 8 feet (2.4 m); width 14 feet (4.3 m), wheel width 4 feet (1.2 m); length 18 feet (5.5 m)', '10 tons', 550, NULL, 'No price printed. Model type is given as the standard chassis for all Warlord sales; used by all Warlord factions.', 'A huge four-wheeled armored infantry vehicle whose blade-studded Mega-Damage wheels grind up whatever it drives over, crewed by a pilot in a sealed cabin slung low between the wheels and a pack of Reavers riding the cage on top. Power: liquid fuel (40%) with a 200 mile (352 km) range, or nuclear (unlimited). Cargo: an external storage bay (60 M.D.C.) for rifles, ammo, grenades and canteens. A rider who falls under the wheels is -4 to dodge or roll. Works well on farmland, towns, light forest, grassland and steppe; struggles on steep rocky ground and cannot enter thick forest. Sensors: HUD, short-range directional communications 20 miles (32 km), bullhorn 400 feet (122 m), mini-radar antenna tracking up to 20 aerial targets, extended radio (adds 20 miles/32 km) and Cyber-Link.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.156-157'),
  ('war-wagon-mechanized-ram', 'War Wagon/Mechanized Ram', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Pilot/Commander, Co-Pilot, Navigator, Communications Officer, Weapons Officer, and one Heavy Machine. Can be driven by a single cybernetically-linked pilot at -20%.', 'Troop complement: up to one platoon of 40 human troops (50 cramped) plus 10 Light Machines and 10 Heavy Machines; or 40 human troops and 25 Light Machines; or 50 Light Machines; or 36 Heavy Machines.', 'Top speed 70 mph (112 km); cruising 40 mph (65 km). Forests and rugged terrain halve maximum speed or worse; dense forest and rocky terrain are impassable.', NULL, NULL, 'Height 17 feet (5 m); width 18 feet (5.5 m); length 90 feet (27.4 m)', '150 tons', 400, NULL, 'No price printed. Used extensively by the Burgasov, Orloff and Sokolov Camps and less so by the Seriyev Camp; the Sovietski has 432 slightly modified ones.', 'A 90-foot armored personnel carrier on six axles of spiked solid wheels, built to breach fortifications with a V-shaped hydraulic ram and to rake both sides of a street with banks of ion turrets. The book prints two main body sections: Forward 400 (stored as the main body, since losing it shuts the vehicle down) and Troop 800; the troop section, its blast doors and rail guns keep working after the forward section is gone, and the wagon is scrap only when both are destroyed. Power: liquid fuel (30%) or nuclear (70%). Cargo: an aft storeroom holding 2 tons; it can carry or pull 110 tons. The prose also seats a field doctor or scientist on the bridge and two gunnery crewmen behind it. Slow and easily stuck in swamp, woodland, deep snow and rough ground; not amphibious. Sensors: Gromeko battle computer, holographic optics, HUD, enviro-360 sensor screens, communications to 100 miles (160 km), radar to 50 miles (90 km) registering up to 64 targets, four spotlights (300 ft/91 m), loudspeaker (600 feet/182.8 m) and Cyber-Links for pilot, co-pilot and gunners. Hydraulic boarding ramps tilt up or down 20 degrees; the rear hatch has no ramp.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.157-159'),
  ('warthrone', 'Warthrone', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Three; a pilot and two gunners, plus the Warlord and his entourage.', 'The Warlord on the throne and room for as many as a dozen others; the usual complement is two guards flanking the throne plus 2-4 advisors, aides or honored heroes. Troop complement: 24-49 escorts, more if the situation calls for it.', NULL, 'Hover to 68 mph (109 km), cruising 40 mph (65 km). Maximum altitude 100 feet (30.5 m); normally hovers 15-20 feet (4.6 to 6 m) above the ground.', NULL, 'Disk 8 feet (2.4 m) thick, canopies two feet above the deck, throne and flagpoles add 15 feet (4.6 m) for an overall height of 23 feet (7 m); 25 feet (7.6 m) in diameter', '23 tons fully loaded', 900, 6000000, 'Warlord cost 6-10 million depending on how ornate the platform is (no unit printed). The low end is stored. Only about 40 exist, all the exclusive property of the Warlords of Russia.', 'A slow, saucer-shaped military hover platform topped by a 15 foot throne, used by a Warlord as a parade stage and mobile command post; each Warlord keeps one plus two spares. Power: nuclear with a 25 year life. Cargo: none by design, though it can handle about nine tons. Losing one hover jet cuts speed 15%, two 30%, four 75% with a wobble, a 20 feet (3 m) altitude limit as printed, and -40% piloting. With a canopy gone, 30 M.D.C. into the control panels fries the controls. The Warlord can fly it and fire the missiles and lasers from panels hidden in the throne, which also holds a first-aid kit, a pair of Vibro-Blades, a Type Two Fusion Block, six grenades and an energy rifle; a floor panel in front of the throne opens onto a 100 M.D.C. hiding chamber. Sensors: Gromeko battle computer, holographic optics, HUD, enviro-360 sensor screens, communications to 100 miles (160 km), radio scrambler, directional laser communication, radar to 50 miles (90 km) registering up to 64 targets, five underbelly spotlights (300 ft/91 m), loudspeaker/P.A. (1000 feet/305 m), language translator, onboard computer and Cyber-Link.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.159-162'),
  ('wingrider-flying-wing', 'Wingrider', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One; pilot.', NULL, 'Not possible, although the ski-like landing gear can slide across ice and deep snow.', 'Top speed 300 mph (482.7 km); cruising typically 100-170 mph (160 to 272 km). Silent gliding at 60 to 160 mph (96.5 to 256 km). Dive 400 mph (643 km). Altitude ceiling 6,000 feet (1,828.8 meters); above it -30% piloting and speed reduced 40%.', NULL, 'Main body about three feet (0.9 m) thick; harness and railings add four feet (printed 1.2 cm) and landing gear another foot, for an overall height of about 7-8 feet (2.1 to 2.4 m); wingspan 18 feet (5.4 m); length 6 feet (1.8 m) at the center tapering to two feet (0.6 m) at the wing tips', '600 pounds (272.2 kg)', 180, 500000, 'Black Market cost 500,000 to 700,000 credits. The low end is stored. Liquid fuel is a rarity (12%); 88% are nuclear.', 'A one-man flying wing (model type Warlord Wingrider) whose pilot hangs in a flexible harness between two rails above twin jets; hard to fly and built of super-light alloys, it is fielded as an elite combat force only by Warlords Romanov, Seriyev and Kolodenko. Range 500 miles at full speed on liquid fuel, unlimited on nuclear; no cargo. Getting airborne uses 3-4 of the pilot''s melee actions. It is -30% to read on radar (Read Sensory Equipment). Attackers are -1 to strike per 50 mph (80 km) of the Wing''s speed on top of the -5 for small targets, so -11 at 300 mph (482 km), and -6 for the Chulki Hooks. Destroying the short/medium-range missile armor gives a 1% chance per point of M.D. from the first missile detonated that all detonate. If the main body is destroyed in flight the pilot falls (1D6 M.D. per 200 feet/61 m). Airborne bonuses, added to the Wingrider Pilot O.C.C.: +1 initiative, +1 parry, +2 dodge. No Cyber-Link is available. Without the Wingrider Power Armor a helmeted pilot is -20% piloting, -3 initiative and loses one melee action, cyborgs excepted.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.162-164'),
  ('wingrider-power-armor', 'Wingrider Power Armor', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One wearer; the Wingrider pilot.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 140, NULL, 'No price printed; the suit comes with the Flying Wing.', 'A sleek, fairly light full environmental exoskeleton issued with the Wingrider Flying Wing, with a wide-vision helmet (often bird styled) and the standard power armor features. It augments P.S. to a robot P.S. of 24. It has a mount for a jet pack, but no backpack of any kind can be worn while piloting the Wingrider. The wearer can also stow an energy weapon on top of the wing beside the railing. The Cyber-Link is not available for it. The block prints only main body and helmet M.D.C., with no speed, size, weight or price.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.164'),
  ('war-knight-power-armor', 'Typical War-Knight Power Armor', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One wearer; a War-Knight.', NULL, 'Running speed is the wearer''s Spd attribute x10. Leaps 10 feet (3 m) high and 15 feet (4.6 m) across; double with a running start.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 130, 1600000, 'Most are provided by the Warlord; those sold on the Black Market go for 1.6 to 2.5 million credits. The low end is stored.', 'The custom full environmental power armor that is standard equipment for the War-Knight O.C.C.; it augments strength and speed but cannot fly, and details vary from knight to knight within the printed ranges. Main body is rolled, 2D6x10+110 (130-230), so the stored figure is the minimum. Worn, it gives a Robot P.S. of 26-30, fatigue at 10% of normal, +1 attack per melee round at levels 2, 6 and 12, +1 initiative, and +1 to strike and parry. Hand to hand damage: restrained punch 1D4 M.D., punch 1D6 M.D., power punch 2D6 M.D. (two attacks), kick 2D4 M.D., leap kick 2D6 M.D., body flip/throw or tackle 1D4 M.D. The location table lists one or two retractable Vibro-Blades and one forearm weapon, but the block prints no damage, range or payload for either.', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.93-94'),
  ('mm-61-explorer-exoframe', 'Minsky Mekanikal MM-61 Explorer Exoframe', 'rifts', 'power-armor', 'One operator: a human in body armor, a Light Machine, or a small human-shaped Heavy Machine cyborg under 7.6 feet/2.3 m tall.', NULL, '25 mph (40 km) on land.', NULL, 'Half land speed underwater; maximum depth 500 feet (152 m), or less depending on what the pilot is wearing.', '12 feet (3.6 m) tall and 6.6 feet (2 m) wide', '2.4 tons', 200, 750000, 'Black Market cost 750,000-900,000 credits for the E-Pack version; 2.8 million credits for the nuclear one (only 15% are nuclear). The low end is stored. Good availability in western Russia, poor in the eastern wilderness.', 'A crude heavy-duty work exoskeleton designed for exploration and now used for construction, repair, mining and cargo hauling. It is not environmental: the operator straps into an open harness seat, shielded by plating at the back and sides and exposed only from the front, so most wear body armor. Robot P.S. of 35. Hand to hand: crush/squeeze 1D4 M.D., restrained punch and stomp 1D4 M.D., full strength punch or kick 2D4 M.D., power punch 3D6 M.D. (two attacks). Power: two E-Packs give 24 hours of use per pair, with a further E-Pack for each arm, tool or weapon; or nuclear. One hydraulic arm (right or left) extends up to an additional 5 feet (1.5 m). Carries one ton plus the pilot and pulls six tons at half speed. Other gear: Cyber-Link, four small spotlights (300 feet/91 m), radio (10 miles/16 km), grapnel and rewind cord (200 feet/60 m).', 'Rifts World Book 17: Warlords of Russia p.176-177');

